   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_CHE_ZURI_08">Universitätstrasse 38 I</name>
                            </addrLine>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_CHE_ZURI">Zurich</name>
                            </addrLine>
                        </address>
                    </ab>
                </div> <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>Dear <name type="person" key="weav76">Miss Weaver</name>: I forgot to say in <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">
                            <rs type="letter" key="L_68473">my letter</rs>
                        </seg> of last night that <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">Messrs Crès</seg> have sold out all their copies of my novel. They have also placed copies of <name type="work" key="W_THEEGOIST">
                            <hi rend="underline">The Egoist</hi>
                        </name> in their public reading room but it would be enough to send one or two for that purpose. If you wish to advertise the paper you could send a copy to the <seg xml:id="seg3" type="note-anchor">Museums-Gesellschaft</seg>. I shall speak to the chairman <name type="person" key="vett53">Professor Vetter</name> who is also rector of the university here and has read my novel. As regards <seg xml:id="seg4" type="note-anchor">Messrs Duckworth</seg> I have to say that my agent offered the book to them. They returned it with a letter so offensive that my agent preferred not to send me a copy of it.  <name type="person" key="poun85">Mr Pound</name>, I believe, saw the letter.</p>
                    <p>        	Excuse this hasty note. I am sorry to say that this morning I have some slight symptoms again in my eye. It may be nothing. I telephoned to my doctor but as it is Whitsuntide he is out of town. I hope it may be nothing.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>        	sincerely yours</salute>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">                    	James Joyce</name>
                        </signed> </closer> </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">JJ's <name type="letter" key="L_68473">letter to Harriet Weaver dated 18 May 1918</name> (<name type="work">LI</name> 113-14).</note>
                <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">Georges-Célestin Crès (1875-1935) was a publisher and bookseller who founded Crès et Cie in Paris in 1913, and in 1916, urged by the French Minister of Foreign Affairs, opened a French bookstore in Zurich. In October 1917, that bookstore agreed to take copies of a new English edition of <name type="work" key="W_APORTRAIT">A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man</name>.</note>
                <note xml:id="note3" target="#seg3" type="commentary">Literary institution, lending library and reading room in Zurich. JJ made frequent use of it.</note>
                <note xml:id="note4" target="#seg4" type="commentary">London publishing firm founded in 1898.</note>
            </div>
